Question #7 * What would happen if you tried cbind(scores, c(85, 91, 100)) — giving 3 values for a matrix that only has 2 rows? * Test it, read the warning/error, and explain why cbind() and rbind()) require matching dimensions.
```{r}
# Try adding a column with 3 values to a matrix with 2 rows
test_scores <- cbind(scores, c(85, 91, 100))

# Display the result
print(test_scores)
```
Answer:When I use cbind() with three values while the original matrix has only two rows, R displays a warning because the dimensions do not match. R tries to recycle the values to fill the available rows, so the third value is not included in the resulting matrix. The cbind() function requires matching row counts, while rbind() requires matching column counts. This ensures that the data is arranged correctly.

Question #8:In what situation would you use rbind() instead of cbind() when updating a dataset over time?
Give a concrete example (e.g., daily sales, weekly attendance) and explain your reasoning.

Answer:I would use rbind() when adding new records or observations to an existing dataset over time. For example, if I have a daily sales record for Monday to Friday and want to add Saturday's sales, I would use rbind() to add a new row. I would use this function because Saturday represents a new observation with the same columns as the previous days. This makes the dataset organized and easier to update.

